 ## One thing I'd change from the original architecture

 Since you're targeting a **junior** position, don't make the application itself unnecessarily complicated.

 You don't need 15 microservices.

 I'd use something like:

```
                Internet
                   │
                   ▼
             AWS Load Balancer
                   │
                   ▼
             React Frontend
                   │
                   ▼
              Backend API
              /         \
             /           \
            ▼             ▼
       PostgreSQL       Redis
```

 Then put the DevOps complexity around the application rather than making the application itself extremely complex.

 Your real learning objective is:

 > **"Can I take an application from source code → secure CI → container → registry → GitOps → Kubernetes → AWS → monitoring → troubleshooting?"**

 If you can genuinely demonstrate that end-to-end, you've got a **very solid junior DevOps portfolio project**.

 And one final piece of advice: **build it yourself rather than copying an existing GitHub repository.** When you eventually get asked _"Why did you do this?"_ in an interview, your ability to explain the decisions and troubleshoot problems will matter much more than the number of technologies in your README.
